        {
          "path": "_m/w/w/7/r/four-gates-pagoda-wp/jinan-2009-1202.jpg",
          "filename": "Jinan 2009 1202.jpg",
          "caption": "Simen Ta. 611 AD, near Jinan City\nThe Four-Entrance Pagoda lies about 36km from Jinan on the road to Mt. Tai. Dated 611 (Sui dynasty) by an inscription on one of the roof beams, this is the oldest stone pagoda in China (but, see below). It is built of ashlar stone blocks, with corbeled eaves. The square plan is about 7m on a side, with a total height of 15m. Built in Kushan style, the stupa is topped with a Sumeru-style harmika; the building as a whole also represents the world mountain.    \nInside, there is a central pillar with four buddhas overlooking the cardinal directions. At least one of these images is 60 years older (ca. 544) than the building itself, according to a now-lost dedicatory inscription.     \nStatements like \"this is the oldest X building in Y\" always have to be read carefully. Burial pagodas do not count, and there is an older brick pagoda (the Song Yue Si Ta, near Dengfeng) in Henan dated 523; so Simen Ta is actually the second-oldest pagoda in China.",
          "alt": "Simen Ta. 611 AD, near Jinan City\nThe Four-Entrance Pagoda lies about 36km from Jinan on the road to Mt. Tai. Dated 611 (Sui dynasty) by an inscription on one of the roof beams, this is the oldest stone pagoda in China (but, see below). It is built of ashlar stone blocks, with corbeled eaves. The square plan is about 7m on a side, with a total height of 15m. Built in Kushan style, the stupa is topped with a Sumeru-style harmika; the building as a whole also represents the world mountain.    \nInside, there is a central pillar with four buddhas overlooking the cardinal directions. At least one of these images is 60 years older (ca. 544) than the building itself, according to a now-lost dedicatory inscription.     \nStatements like \"this is the oldest X building in Y\" always have to be read carefully. Burial pagodas do not count, and there is an older brick pagoda (the Song Yue Si Ta, near Dengfeng) in Henan dated 523; so Simen Ta is actually the second-oldest pagoda in China.",
          "width": 1944,
          "height": 2592,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
